            <ArticleTitle>Unilateral congenital ovarian and partial tubal absence: report of four cases with review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>936.e5-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2008.09.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present four rare cases of congenital unilateral tubal and ovarian abnormalities with their laparoscopic images.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Baskent University Faculty of Medicine, Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Konya, Turkey.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Three patients with infertility showing abnormality on hysterosalpingography and a fourth patient with acute pelvic pain who were admitted to our clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">All four patients underwent laparoscopic surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case 1 showed an absence of a distal tubal segment with ipsilateral ovarian agenesis. Case 2 showed a unilateral partial distal tubal absence with normal adjacent ovary. Case 3 showed a twisted and adherent tube with ipsilateral ovarian absence. Case 4 showed a unilateral ovarian agenesis and contralateral acute adnexal torsion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We detected four cases of unilateral ovarian and partial tubal absence in these operations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We present four unusual cases of unilateral ovarian and partial tubal absence. The absence of one ovary and tube may be explained by two possible etiopathogenic causes: asymptomatic adnexial torsion or congenital malformation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
